  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Mobs and Microbes is showing early scholarly and practical reach through 1,020 recorded downloads, indicating that the book is being actively consulted. Its subject matter also aligns closely with Sustainable Development Goal 11, Sustainable Cities and Communities, reflecting relevance to urban history, public health, and the governance of shared spaces.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published by Leuven University Press in 2023, Mobs and Microbes examines market halls as sites where architecture, urbanism, sanitation, political life, and social unrest intersect. That interdisciplinary framing is reinforced by the record: the book is associated with urbanism, architecture, sanitation, politics, economy, and related fields, placing it at the intersection of humanities and social-science inquiry with clear policy resonance for city-making and public health.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest evidence of uptake so far is usage. The book has accumulated 1,020 downloads through OAPEN, suggesting that readers are finding and accessing it beyond the immediate publisher audience. The presence of an open-access instance helps explain that visibility, while the monthly download pattern shows sustained interest rather than a single isolated spike.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The record also indicates institutional support from KU Leuven, Toronto Metropolitan University, and University College Dublin, which strengthens the book\u2019s scholarly grounding and points to collaborative international engagement behind the project. A peer-review signal is present as well, supporting the book\u2019s academic credibility within its publishing context.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Citation uptake has not yet emerged in the OpenAlex record, so the impact story is currently driven more by access and relevance than by citation accumulation. That is still meaningful for a scholarly book in an area where historians, urbanists, architects, and policy-oriented readers may engage the work first through reading and teaching rather than immediate citation. Overall, the record portrays a book with clear thematic significance, open availability, and growing readership in a field with strong connections to sustainable urban futures.</p>",
